      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "To solve this problem, we need to use the concept of inverse variation. When two variables are inversely proportional, their product remains constant. Let's denote the force needed to loosen the bolt as \\( F \\) and the length of the handle of the wrench as \\( L \\). According to the problem, \\( F \\) is inversely proportional to \\( L \\), which means:\n\n\\[ F \\times L = k \\]\n\nwhere \\( k \\) is a constant. We are given that a wrench with a handle length of 9 inches requires 375 pounds of force to loosen the bolt. So, we can write:\n\n\\[ 375 \\times 9 = k \\]\n\nNow, we need to find the force required for a wrench with a handle length of 15 inches. Let's call this force \\( F' \\). Using the same constant \\( k \\), we have:\n\n\\[ F' \\times 15 = k \\]\n\nSince \\( k \\) is the same in both cases, we can set the two equations equal to each other:\n\n\\[ 375 \\times 9 = F' \\times 15 \\]\n\nNow, we can solve for \\( F' \\):\n\n\\[ F' = \\frac{375 \\times 9}{15} \\]\n\n\\[ F' = \\frac{3375}{15} \\]\n\n\\[ F' = 225 \\]\n\nSo, a wrench with a handle length of 15 inches will require 225 pounds of force to loosen the same bolt.",
